WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.512
The small PyTorch example exposes the core attention calculation without hiding it inside a library class.

00:00:07.512 --> 00:00:10.112
We manually create tiny Q, K, and V tensors.

00:00:10.112 --> 00:00:16.192
The matrix multiplication between Q and the transpose of K produces the raw compatibility scores.

00:00:16.192 --> 00:00:23.817
Dividing by the square root of the feature dimension performs the scaling step.

00:00:23.817 --> 00:00:28.232
Softmax converts each row into normalized attention weights.

00:00:28.232 --> 00:00:34.047
The final matrix multiplication between those weights and V produces the context vectors.

00:00:34.047 --> 00:00:37.337
Run the cell and print every intermediate object.

00:00:37.337 --> 00:00:42.972
Look at one row of the weight matrix and verify that its values sum to approximately one.

00:00:42.972 --> 00:00:46.777
Then change one Query vector and see how the weights move.

00:00:46.777 --> 00:00:50.517
This is deliberately not a complete Transformer layer.

00:00:50.517 --> 00:00:57.392
There are no learned Q, K, V projections, no multiple heads, no residual connection, no normalization,

00:00:57.392 --> 00:00:58.852
and no feed-forward network.

00:00:58.852 --> 00:01:06.444
The goal is to make the central calculation transparent before we return to the full architecture.
